  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing the minimum probabilities of each lottery, which in this context—since each lottery has only two outcomes—corresponds exactly to the probability of the better (non-zero) outcome. Thus, the 'minimum probability' as defined by the reason aligns with the lower of the two probabilities in each lottery, which is 25% for A and 20% for B. The individual also focuses on the probability of winning (non-zero outcome), which is the same value (25% vs. 20%), and explicitly states a preference for the higher one. Although the individual does not use the technical term 'minimum probability', their reasoning process—evaluating and preferring the option with the higher chance of a positive outcome—matches the rule of the decision reason. The relevant information (probabilities of winning) is the same as that required by the reason, and the justification (preferring higher probability of gain) aligns with the reason’s rule. Therefore, it is highly likely the individual used this decision reason, even if implicitly.",
  "final_assessment_score": 100
}

